WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.463 --> 00:00:07.966
Basic science represents curiosity.

00:00:09.009 --> 00:00:11.177
We decided to break it up into distinct disciplines

00:00:11.177 --> 00:00:12.470
like biology,

00:00:12.470 --> 00:00:14.139
chemistry and astronomy.

00:00:14.139 --> 00:00:15.223
But ultimately,

00:00:15.223 --> 00:00:17.976
it's all the same kind of curiosity,

00:00:17.976 --> 00:00:19.853
which is how do things work?

00:00:19.853 --> 00:00:22.105
How did they begin? How do they end?

00:00:22.105 --> 00:00:23.857
That is essential

00:00:23.857 --> 00:00:26.776
to the nature of what it means to be human.

00:00:26.776 --> 00:00:29.112
Science is about thinking

00:00:29.112 --> 00:00:30.613
and new ways of seeing things.

00:00:30.613 --> 00:00:31.448
Maybe there are things

00:00:31.448 --> 00:00:34.576
that we've actually described in the same way

00:00:34.576 --> 00:00:36.036
for a thousand years,

00:00:36.036 --> 00:00:37.328
and then all of a sudden

00:00:37.328 --> 00:00:40.415
we find some discovery or some realization

00:00:40.415 --> 00:00:43.418
that says we've got to completely rethink

00:00:43.418 --> 00:00:45.795
the way that we actually consider this.

00:00:48.715 --> 00:00:51.426
Science does that more succinctly

00:00:51.426 --> 00:00:53.762
than almost any other kind of human endeavor.

00:00:53.762 --> 00:00:54.929
And so every bit as much

00:00:54.929 --> 00:00:57.766
as we appreciate art or poetry,

00:00:57.766 --> 00:01:01.311
we appreciate science.

00:01:01.311 --> 00:01:05.523
Yale, it's an absolutely fabulous place for scholarship.

00:01:05.523 --> 00:01:07.317
Its history is based on

00:01:07.317 --> 00:01:08.610
learning and discovery.

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:11.404
That's why we're here.

00:01:11.404 --> 00:01:13.198
Yale is an intellectual hub.

00:01:13.198 --> 00:01:14.532
It is a place that has

00:01:14.532 --> 00:01:18.036
a strong legacy of originating

00:01:18.036 --> 00:01:21.664
some of the most exciting new ideas.

00:01:21.664 --> 00:01:25.210
Our mission is to create new knowledge

00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:27.587
and to transmit that knowledge

00:01:27.587 --> 00:01:28.546
through our teaching.

00:01:28.546 --> 00:01:30.799
The thrill of saying,

00:01:30.799 --> 00:01:32.008
"I have this question

00:01:32.008 --> 00:01:33.676
about how the world works.

00:01:33.676 --> 00:01:35.345
Nobody knows the answer.

00:01:35.345 --> 00:01:36.429
I'm going to go out

00:01:36.429 --> 00:01:38.765
and I'm going to design the experiment

00:01:38.765 --> 00:01:39.766
or collect the data

00:01:39.766 --> 00:01:40.892
or develop the theory

00:01:40.892 --> 00:01:42.435
that answers that question

00:01:42.435 --> 00:01:44.145
and that pushes the boundaries

00:01:44.145 --> 00:01:45.230
of human knowledge."

00:01:45.230 --> 00:01:47.565
There's nothing like it.

00:01:47.565 --> 00:01:49.901
Much of science is laborious.

00:01:49.901 --> 00:01:51.945
It's called a 'laboratory' for a reason.

00:01:52.237 --> 00:01:54.906
And it's also very time-consuming.

00:01:54.906 --> 00:01:55.782
It takes a while

00:01:55.782 --> 00:01:57.575
before we can actually see the importance

00:01:57.575 --> 00:01:59.619
of these things that we do.

00:01:59.619 --> 00:02:01.538
And in fact, what really is happening

00:02:01.538 --> 00:02:03.998
is that we're doing the slow process

00:02:03.998 --> 00:02:05.208
of thinking things through.

00:02:06.126 --> 00:02:07.836
Science is unpredictable.

00:02:07.836 --> 00:02:10.171
And I'd like to think that institutions

00:02:10.171 --> 00:02:11.256
such as ours

00:02:11.256 --> 00:02:13.383
offer the space for the more creative,

00:02:13.383 --> 00:02:14.884
unpredictable kind of work

00:02:14.884 --> 00:02:15.760
where we don't know

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:17.220
what the outcome is going to be.

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:19.305
You want to be fearless

00:02:19.305 --> 00:02:22.183
and not constrained by current thinking.

00:02:22.183 --> 00:02:23.393
If we're curious,

00:02:23.393 --> 00:02:25.353
but we're not actually ambitious

00:02:25.353 --> 00:02:26.604
and we're not brave enough

00:02:26.604 --> 00:02:29.232
to sort of step outside of our comfort zone,

00:02:29.232 --> 00:02:31.568
then in fact, we probably won't succeed

00:02:31.568 --> 00:02:33.027
in making real progress.

00:02:34.112 --> 00:02:37.866
The big leaps forward, the big discoveries,

00:02:37.866 --> 00:02:39.367
they often start

00:02:39.367 --> 00:02:42.954
by someone who had a very specific question

00:02:42.954 --> 00:02:46.457
about a very specific system or process

00:02:46.457 --> 00:02:48.960
and in the course of answering that,

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:51.212
opened up this whole new vista for us.

00:02:51.212 --> 00:02:53.506
These projects are very granular,

00:02:53.506 --> 00:02:56.843
but that is how the frontiers of knowledge expand.

00:02:58.595 --> 00:02:59.929
When you're the first one

00:02:59.929 --> 00:03:01.139
to actually see something

00:03:01.139 --> 00:03:02.974
and you know that you're the first human being

00:03:02.974 --> 00:03:05.143
to know that this is how it works...

00:03:05.143 --> 00:03:07.187
That's an incomparable experience.

00:03:07.187 --> 00:03:08.021
It's exciting.

00:03:08.021 --> 00:03:09.647
It's really incredible

00:03:09.647 --> 00:03:11.941
to have the opportunity to do what I do.

00:03:12.650 --> 00:03:15.069
But it also tends to have, years down the road,

00:03:15.069 --> 00:03:16.779
these unexpected payoffs

00:03:16.779 --> 00:03:19.240
in which people had no idea

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:20.867
that this simple discovery was going to

00:03:20.867 --> 00:03:23.077
actually be applied in an area

00:03:23.077 --> 00:03:25.121
that's very, very different than what they

00:03:25.121 --> 00:03:25.788
had assumed.

00:03:25.788 --> 00:03:27.707
Without the foundational work

00:03:27.707 --> 00:03:29.042
that comes out of basic sciences,

00:03:29.042 --> 00:03:30.376
there is no applied science.

00:03:30.376 --> 00:03:31.502
There is no technology.

00:03:32.420 --> 00:03:34.881
Curiosity-driven research at Yale and beyond

00:03:34.881 --> 00:03:36.466
has underpinned

00:03:36.466 --> 00:03:38.676
almost every technological advance

00:03:38.676 --> 00:03:39.385
that you can think of,

00:03:39.385 --> 00:03:43.932
from food production to heating and cooling, to GPS,

00:03:43.932 --> 00:03:46.851
to diagnostic medical imaging.

00:03:46.851 --> 00:03:49.979
We're talking about scientific research.

00:03:49.979 --> 00:03:51.397
What we're actually talking about is

00:03:51.397 --> 00:03:53.233
an investment into the future.

00:03:53.233 --> 00:03:55.068
It's an almost guaranteed thing.

00:03:55.068 --> 00:03:57.028
People understand that

00:03:57.028 --> 00:03:58.446
the stock market goes up and down,

00:03:58.446 --> 00:03:59.948
but generally over time

00:03:59.948 --> 00:04:01.449
your investment is going to pay off

00:04:01.449 --> 00:04:03.743
and that is certainly the case with science.

00:04:03.743 --> 00:04:06.246
A basic scientist tries to understand

00:04:06.246 --> 00:04:07.247
the world we live in.

00:04:07.247 --> 00:04:08.414
We seek the truth,

00:04:08.414 --> 00:04:09.874
we seek knowledge

00:04:09.874 --> 00:04:12.377
because there's just so much out there

00:04:12.377 --> 00:04:13.127
to know.

00:04:13.127 --> 00:04:14.545
Right now,

00:04:14.545 --> 00:04:16.714
in Yale's Faculty of Arts and Sciences,

00:04:16.714 --> 00:04:18.383
fundamental research is being done

00:04:18.383 --> 00:04:20.468
that will end up transforming

00:04:20.468 --> 00:04:23.388
our understanding of human health and disease,

00:04:23.388 --> 00:04:24.764
of neuroscience,

00:04:24.764 --> 00:04:26.432
of the quantum world,

00:04:26.432 --> 00:04:28.226
and beyond.

